<gpx version="1.1" creator="Good Shit" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1"><trk><name>Senorady–Oslava canyon–Velká skála circuit — Senorady, Oslava mills, Velká skála and Salaš circuit [planning only — not navigation-grade]</name><desc>From the public Senorady village walking junction, follow the yellow-marked approach to Senoradský mlýn and the narrow rocky Oslava riverside ground. Continue on the green-marked mill and bridge approach to Ketkovský mlýn, return over the same bridge ground, and take the yellow approach to the actual upper Velká skála walking viewpoint ground. Complete the adopted Salaš walking return into Senorady. The complete same-named circuit is approximately 6.6 km; yellow and green marked legs and the Salaš and village junctions require route attention.

This is demanding hiking: sections of the rocky riverside path are only about 0.2 m wide, with unguarded exposure up to about 12 m above the river. There is no handrail on the exposed ledges: a fall can cause serious injury or death. This exposure comes from source mapping, not a current field survey. Steep slopes, boulders, cliff margins, narrow ledges and steps require secure footing and careful route attention; it is unsuitable for walkers uncomfortable with exposure.

Prefer dry spring or autumn daylight. Postpone in high water, ice, rain or saturated mud; wet rock and exposed river sections can be unsafe. Check current weather, river, path and bridge conditions.

Stay on the established walking grounds: follow the yellow and green river/mill legs and the adopted rock and Salaš paths, protect fragile vegetation and obey current signs and lawful restrictions. Do not shortcut slopes, climb outside the walking rock ground or enter private mill interiors.

Postpone the entire itinerary if any required passage or bridge is closed, prohibited, unsafe or unusable. Dogs must remain controlled and leashed where required; avoid disturbing wildlife or damaging habitats.

Inherited 385 m ascent, 385 m descent and 270 minutes are approximate planning values, without a measured elevation profile; allow extra time for difficult footing and stops.

